BODY = '''<h2>What MedlinePlus, the CDC and the ADA Say</h2>
<p>MedlinePlus, in its "Bathroom safety for adults" page (reviewed April 2026), recommends two grab bars in the tub area, one for getting in and out of the tub and one for standing from a seated position. It says to fix them to the wall vertically or horizontally and not diagonally, and warns that towel racks are not grab bars because they can't support your weight.</p>
<p>The CDC's "Check for Safety" checklist asks whether you need support getting in and out of the tub or up from the toilet, and says to have grab bars put in next to and inside the tub and next to the toilet. The CDC also says more than 1 in 4 older adults falls each year, and that a single fall doubles the chance of another.</p>
<p>The 2010 ADA Standards, published by the U.S. Access Board, give measured numbers for grab bars in public buildings. A round bar should be 1-1/4 to 2 inches in diameter, sit 1-1/2 inches off the wall, and a horizontal bar should be 33 to 36 inches above the finish floor. The bar, its fasteners and the wall behind it must hold a 250-pound force at any point, and the bar must not rotate in its fittings. A private home does not have to follow the ADA, but these numbers are a useful reference.</p>
<p class="note"><strong>The wall is the weak point, not the bar.</strong> Moen and Amazon Basics tie their 500-pound ratings to a stud or another secure mounting. Find the studs with a stud finder, screw into them, and use the maker's anchors only where there is no stud. If you are unsure, ask someone who installs them.</p>

<h2>How to Choose a Grab Bar</h2>
<h3>Where to put them</h3>
<p>Follow MedlinePlus and the CDC: one bar to help you step in and out of the tub, one to help you stand up in it, and one next to the toilet. Fix them vertically or horizontally, not diagonally.</p>
<h3>Length</h3>
<p>A short bar, like the 12-inch Moen, gives one place to grab. A 24 or 36-inch bar gives a path along the wall. The picks run from 12 to 36 inches.</p>
<h3>Diameter</h3>
<p>The ADA allows 1-1/4 to 2 inches for round bars. The Moen 24 and the 36-inch Amazon Basics are 1.25 inches, and the Moen 12 and 18-inch Amazon Basics are 1.5 inches. Choose the one that fits your hand.</p>
<h3>Grip surface</h3>
<p>A smooth stainless bar can be slippery when wet. The Gotega has a knurled grip, and the others are brushed or smooth stainless.</p>
<h3>Mounting</h3>
<p>Read the load rating with its condition. Moen's bar says it needs a stud or SecureMounts for 500 pounds, Amazon Basics says a stud or other secure surface, and the Gotega names no condition. Suction-cup bars are a different product, and none of our picks is one.</p>

<h2>Grab Bar Questions</h2>
<h3>How many grab bars do I need?</h3>
<p>MedlinePlus says two in the tub area: one for getting in and out of the tub and one for standing up from a seated position. The CDC's checklist adds grab bars next to the toilet. Fix them to the wall vertically or horizontally and not diagonally, as MedlinePlus says, and choose lengths that suit the wall: a 24-inch bar for a vertical grip at the tub's edge, for example, and a longer one for a horizontal run.</p>
<h3>Can I use a towel rack as a grab bar?</h3>
<p>No. MedlinePlus says plainly not to use towel racks as grab bars because they can't support your weight. A towel rack is fixed with small screws into tile or drywall, and a grab bar has to be fixed into a stud or another secure mounting. The same goes for soap dishes and the edge of a sink. Fit a real grab bar next to the tub and the toilet.</p>
<h3>How high should a grab bar be?</h3>
<p>The ADA Standards put a horizontal bar 33 to 36 inches above the finished floor. That applies to public buildings, but it is a good starting point at home, adjusted to the user's height.</p>
<h3>Do I have to screw it into a stud?</h3>
<p>The makers' 500-pound ratings are tied to the mounting. Moen's listing says 500 pounds when installed into a stud or with its SecureMount anchors, which are sold separately, and Amazon Basics says a wall stud or another secure mounting surface. If you cannot find a stud where you want the bar, use the maker's anchors and follow the instructions in the box exactly.</p>
<h3>Are ADA grab bars required in a home?</h3>
<p>No. The ADA Standards, published by the U.S. Access Board, govern public and commercial buildings and do not bind a private home. They are still a useful reference because they give measured numbers: a round bar 1-1/4 to 2 inches in diameter, 1-1/2 inches from the wall, 33 to 36 inches above the floor for a horizontal bar, strong enough for a 250-pound force at any point.</p>
<h3>Will a stainless steel bar rust in the shower?</h3>
<p>Moen's listing says its bar is corrosion-resistant stainless steel with a limited lifetime warranty, and the Amazon Basics listings say the bars are designed to resist rust and corrosion. Stainless steel is the usual choice for a wet room. Wipe the bar and check the screws or covers from time to time, and replace any bar with a loose mount or visible damage.</p>

<h2>Our Take</h2>
<p>The Moen Home Care 24-inch bar is the best pick. Moen's page states the diameter, the 500-pound load and a limited lifetime warranty, and 6,092 reviews average 4.8 stars. Plan for a stud, or for the SecureMount anchors, which cost extra.</p>
<p>The Amazon Basics 36-inch bar is the one to buy for a long wall, and the 18-inch bar gives you a thicker 1.5-inch grip for a short vertical position. Both have a 1-year warranty and listing-only specs. The Moen 12-inch is a cheap second bar, but its listing gives no weight rating.</p>
<p>The Gotega pack gives you the two bars MedlinePlus advises for the price of one Moen, but its listing names no mounting condition and it has the lowest rating of the five. Fit it into studs, not plugs.</p>

<h2>The Bottom Line</h2>
<p>The Moen Home Care 24-inch bar is the best all-round grab bar. Choose the Amazon Basics 36-inch bar for a long wall and the 18-inch for a short vertical one. Put two in the tub area and one by the toilet, screw into studs and never use a towel rack, as MedlinePlus advises. See also our guides to <a href="/best-shower-chairs/">shower chairs</a> and <a href="/best-raised-toilet-seats/">raised toilet seats</a>.</p>'''

